>>
endobj
423 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 265 0 R
/E (Education \(p= 0.0005, SMD = 0.26\) is significant as well as nopart \(p = 0.003, SMD = -0.207\). The education finding suggests that P3 respondents have meaningfully highereducation than P1 respondents. The SMD is well above the threshold, signaling severe imbalance. Education is controlled for in the OLS models, which partially addresses this, but the magnitude of the differences suggests the two periods are drawing from compositionally different populations the covariate adjustment may not fully eliminate. Non-partisan respondents are overrepresented again in P1 compared to P3, with the SMD also crossing the threshold for imbalance. It is not controlled in the main models. )
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
424 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 266 0 R
/T (Balance Checks)
/E (Balance Checks)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
425 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 266 0 R
/T (P2 v. P3 \(Post-Election vs. Post- Protest\) )
/E (P2 v. P3 \(Post-Election vs. Post- Protest\) )
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
426 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 266 0 R
/E (Welath \(p = 0.002, SMD = 0.242\) reaches signifiance. P3 respondents are substantially welathier than P2 respondants. Wealth is controlled in the models, which mitigates this, but the magnitude is the strongest individaul balance failure in the analysis. Education \(p = 0.020, SMD = 0.155\) is also imbalanced, showing P3 is more educated than P2, but controlled in the models. This comparison is cleaner than P1 vs. P3 and P1 vs. P2 on most conditions. )
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
