>>
endobj
433 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 268 0 R
/T (Code Integrity of The Original)
/E (Code Integrity of The Original)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
434 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 758 0 R
/E (The variable ur is built by adding together two separate questions: which party do you prefer, and which party did you vote for. If a respondent says United Russia for both questions they score 2. If they only answer one they score 1. If neither they score 0.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
435 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 759 0 R
/E (The problem is that when this variable gets multiplied by period3 to create the interaction term, a respondent scoring 2 has twice the weight of a respondent scoring 1. So the interaction coefficient is not actually telling you "how did UR supporters respond differently to the protest compared to non-supporters." It is telling you "what happens to trust for each one unit increase in the ur scale," which is a much less interpretable quantity.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
436 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 760 0 R
/E (In practice for this dataset it turned out that no respondents actually scored 2, so the variable ended up being effectively binary anyway. But the construction is still conceptually wrong and would have been a real problem if the data had behaved the way the code assumed it could.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
